detailedExplanation.whatItIs:
An interface and an implicit implementer:
type Writer interface {
    Write(p []byte) (int, error)
}
// Any type with this exact method satisfies Writer,
// without saying 'implements Writer' anywhere.
func save(w Writer) { w.Write([]byte("data")) }

A classic example:
type MyErr struct{}
func (*MyErr) Error() string { return "boom" }

func do() error {
    var p *MyErr = nil
    return p          // returns interface with type *MyErr, value nil
}
fmt.Println(do() == nil)  // false, not what you expect

Struct embedding promotes fields and methods:
type Animal struct{ Name string }
func (a Animal) Speak() string { return a.Name }

type Dog struct {
    Animal   // embedded, no field name
    Breed string
}
d := Dog{Animal{"Rex"}, "Lab"}
d.Speak()    // promoted from Animal

Using sync.Once for lazy init:
var once sync.Once
var conn *DB

func getConn() *DB {
    once.Do(func() {
        conn = connect()   // runs only once, ever
    })
    return conn
}

Recovering from a panic:
func safeRun() {
    defer func() {
        if r := recover(); r != nil {
            fmt.Println("recovered:", r)
        }
    }()
    panic("something broke")
}

The classic loop-capture problem:
funcs := []func(){}
for i := 0; i < 3; i++ {
    funcs = append(funcs, func() { fmt.Println(i) })
}
// Pre-1.22: prints 3, 3, 3 (shared i)
// Go 1.22+: prints 0, 1, 2 (per-iteration i)

A generic function with a constraint:
type Number interface { ~int | ~float64 }

func Sum[T Number](nums []T) T {
    var total T
    for _, n := range nums {
        total += n
    }
    return total
}
Sum works for any int- or float64-based type.

Struct tags drive JSON mapping:
type User struct {
    Name  string `json:"name"`
    Email string `json:"email,omitempty"`
    pw    string // unexported, ignored by JSON
}
data, _ := json.Marshal(User{Name: "Ada"})
// {"name":"Ada"}

A leaking goroutine blocked forever:
func leak() {
    ch := make(chan int)
    go func() {
        val := <-ch   // blocks forever, no one sends
        fmt.Println(val)
    }()
    // function returns, goroutine stuck, leaked
}
